in @prism
in @glass
in @meta
in @spectral
in @spectral/gen_prism
in @uuid/spectral
in @epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian

# @spectral/entanglement — the peer-correlation edge type.
#
# THE SIXTH SUB-SHARD of the @spectral runtime cascade. Sibling of
# `shards/spectral/gen_prism.mirror` (commit ae965ca, the worker
# primitive whose state surface participates in the restriction map
# this edge carries), `shards/spectral/supervisor.mirror` (commit
# 452ccb2, the lifecycle-owner — orthogonal to this edge class),
# `shards/spectral/registry.mirror` (commit 489211e, the supervisor's
# parentage-side child index whose typed surface stays at the
# parentage discipline; the sheaf reading was deferred TO HERE),
# `shards/spectral/parent.mirror` (commit 9d905a2, the ORTHOGONAL
# sibling — single-parent acyclic lifecycle edge; this shard names
# the OTHER axis of the two-axis substrate graph), and
# `shards/spectral/root.mirror` (commit f145e48, the parentless
# supervisor specialisation whose entanglement participation is
# itself one of this edge class's members at the root altitude).
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §4 (2026-06-10, Mara):
# entanglement is the peer-correlation edge class — ORTHOGONAL to
# parentage. A gen_prism may be entangled with N peers. Entanglement
# is polyadic (an edge spans any subset of gen_prisms that observe
# a shared state projection), cyclic (peers may mutually entangle),
# and observable via mirror-query at substrate altitude.
#
# === The load-bearing recognition: entanglement IS a sheaf restriction ===
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §4 and [[Eigenboard is a sheaf]]:
#
#   "Entanglement edges ARE sheaf restriction maps at substrate
#    altitude. The substrate's eigenboard is a cellular sheaf on the
#    five-operation graph; the conductivity tensor IS the
#    restriction-map structure. Two gen_prisms are entangled iff
#    their state surfaces participate in a shared restriction map —
#    a single observation of one's state induces a measurable
#    projection on the other's."
#
# The substrate ALREADY HAS the restriction primitive at
# `shards/epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian.mirror` (commit predating
# this tick; the 18th-instance rug-pull recognized that Leray's
# `restriction`, Hilbert's `operator`, and Cauchy's `eigenvalue` were
# named at the math altitude). That declaration is at the EIGENBOARD
# altitude — u32-indexed vertices of the cellular sheaf's base graph,
# `weight: ref` magnitudes of the connection 1-form's parallel-transport
# operator. This shard names the SAME structural object at the
# RUNTIME altitude — uuid_spectral-identified gen_prisms as the
# vertices, the restriction map between gen_prism state surfaces as
# the edge data. The math altitude's `restriction` is what the
# realisation layer assembles when the eigenboard reads through to
# the conductivity tensor; the runtime altitude's `restriction_map`
# is what the substrate names as the edge's substrate-altitude carrier.
#
# This is the rug-pull pattern (#55 instance count, NOW 56 with this
# tick): the substrate already had the WORD (restriction) at the math
# altitude; the runtime altitude lifts the same word as its
# substrate-pull-correct edge data.
#
# === The keyword decision ===
#
# `glass` per recognition #46 (path-syntax property; promoted) and
# `shards/spectral.mirror`'s discipline section: "@spectral
# sub-shards at depth >= 1 use `glass`; the family root itself uses
# `prism` per the depth-0 discipline."
#
# Why NOT a new substrate keyword: entanglement is an EDGE TYPE —
# a typed correlation between gen_prisms — and `glass` already names
# that at the sub-prism altitude per `shards/glass.mirror`'s
# `glass(name) -> prism` form. Same reasoning as gen_prism (ae965ca),
# supervisor (452ccb2), registry (489211e), parent (9d905a2), and
# root (f145e48): not a new structural kind, but a typed edge over
# existing carriers (uuid_spectral endpoints, sheaf_laplacian's
# restriction structure).
#
# === The polyadic decision (STOP-condition 2, resolved) ===
#
# Brief STOP-condition 2: should polyadic edges want a different
# vocabulary than binary parent_edges (e.g., `hyper_edge` vs `edge`)?
#
# Substrate-pull-correct resolution: NO new vocabulary. The substrate's
# graph-edge altitude carries TWO edge classes, distinguished by
# TYPE not by VOCABULARY:
#
#   parent_edge      — `{ child: uuid_spectral, parent: uuid_spectral }`.
#                      Binary by record shape. Acyclic. Lifecycle.
#   entanglement_edge — `{ peers: [uuid_spectral], restriction_map: ... }`.
#                      Polyadic by record shape (list of peers).
#                      Cyclic permitted. Correlation.
#
# The polyadicity is named STRUCTURALLY by the record shape (a list
# instead of two named slots), not by a new `hyper_edge` keyword.
# This is the substrate's discipline: introduce vocabulary only when
# a new structural KIND exists; a polyadic edge is the same
# structural kind (graph edge between gen_prisms) as a binary edge,
# just with arity declared at the record level. Adding a `hyper_edge`
# would proliferate discriminators when the list-vs-pair distinction
# already names the arity.
#
# Per [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]]: the algebra is
# closed; arity is a record-shape fact, not an axiomatic kind.
#
# The minimum-peers invariant (>= 2; an edge with one peer is not
# an edge) is NOT carried in the type at this tick — it surfaces as
# a substrate-pull-candidate property/fracture bilateral pair
# (`entanglement_arity_well_formed` at @epistemologic/property +
# fracture body at @mirror/fracture, analogous to parent_acyclic +
# parent_cycle landed this same tick at 0921dca + 20fcde5). The
# property/fracture pair is FORWARD-PROMISED (STOP-condition 4
# below), not landed here, because this tick is the substrate
# type declaration; the bilateral pair is a separate substrate-pull
# instance at the graph-edge altitude.
#
# === The cyclic decision (STOP-condition 3, resolved) ===
#
# Brief STOP-condition 3: does entanglement's cyclic-permitted
# semantic create substrate invariant tensions with other shards
# (e.g., problems with kintsugi loop's settlement)?
#
# Substrate-pull-correct resolution: NO tension. The kintsugi loop
# operates on the FORM-SIDE (state observation; per
# [[architecture-form-process-partition-at-family-root]] candidate
# #55). The entanglement edge IS form-side too — it observes
# correlation; it does not transform. Settlement is over content-
# addressed shards (per [[architecture-shard-as-crdt]]'s bounded
# semilattice); a cycle in the entanglement edge graph does NOT
# create a cycle in shard ascent (shards are content-addressed,
# byte-equal shards collapse). The cyclic entanglement graph is
# OBSERVATIONAL — Mara's spawn entangles with mirror repo's
# gen_prism (Mara reads mirror's substrate); mirror repo's gen_prism
# entangles with Mara's spawn (mirror reads Mara's commits in the
# eigenboard). Symmetric mutual entanglement is the COMMON CASE,
# not the edge case.
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §9 open question 3: "the
# eigenboard query surface currently models entanglement queries
# as forward-traversal — will the existing implementation handle
# cycles, or does this tick imply an eigenboard query lift?" This
# is the runtime-query-implementation question, NOT a substrate
# invariant question. The substrate-altitude type declaration is
# cycle-permissive by construction; whether the eigenboard query
# surface traverses cycles is a downstream consumer question that
# pulls at the consumer altitude (the eigenboard's MCP surface or
# any future query traversal in `@spectral/db`).
#
# === Sheaf-math primitives (STOP-condition 1, resolved) ===
#
# Brief STOP-condition 1: if cellular sheaf math (restriction maps,
# stalk projections, sheaf cohomology) requires substrate primitives
# at `@epistemologic/math/sheaf` that aren't declared yet — STOP.
#
# Substrate-pull-correct resolution: the math primitive is ALREADY
# declared at `shards/epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian.mirror`
# (`type restriction`, `type operator`, `type eigenvalue`, with
# `sheaf_laplacian` and `lambda_zero` actions). The math layer's
# `restriction` carries (source: u32, target: u32, weight: ref) at
# the audible-altitude floor; the runtime layer's `restriction_map`
# specializes to (source_stalk, target_stalk, correlation) over
# uuid_spectral-keyed gen_prism state surfaces.
#
# The two are NOT competing declarations. The math layer's
# `restriction` is the in-a-gauge matrix-block carrier at the
# eigenboard's u32-indexed base graph; the runtime layer's
# `restriction_map` is the substrate-altitude EDGE DATA between
# gen_prism state surfaces. When the eigenboard assembles the
# conductivity tensor, the realisation layer maps gen_prism
# uuid_spectrals to u32 vertex indices and emits the math layer's
# `restriction` records as the assembled `operator`'s entries. The
# substrate-altitude type stays at the gen_prism endpoints; the
# realisation layer indexes them.
#
# This is consistent with parent.mirror's discipline: the
# parent_edge record at the SUBSTRATE altitude carries uuid_spectral
# endpoints; the math-altitude graph-theoretic acyclicity check
# (e.g., topological sort over a u32-indexed adjacency list) lives
# at the realisation layer. Substrate names what the edge IS
# substantively; the realisation names how the structural check is
# computed.
#
# What is NOT lifted in this tick (forward-promised): sheaf
# cohomology actions (H^0, H^1; the substrate has no `cohomology`
# action yet). When the eigenboard's MCP query surface or
# `@spectral/db`'s traversal needs cohomology readings (e.g., for
# global section discovery or obstruction detection), the math
# layer will gain `cohomology(operator) -> [section]` at a separate
# sub-shard. NOT load-bearing for the entanglement edge type
# declaration; the type carries the restriction_map; the cohomology
# computation reads through it at consumer pull.
#
# === The entanglement_edge shape ===
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §4 and
# [[Eigenboard is a sheaf]]:
#
#   peers           — `[uuid_spectral]`. The list of gen_prism
#                     identities participating in this entanglement.
#                     Polyadic: at least two peers (the bilateral
#                     property/fracture pair is forward-promised);
#                     the common case is 2 (binary symmetric
#                     entanglement: Mara's spawn ↔ mirror repo's
#                     gen_prism); larger n names a SHARED state
#                     projection across n gen_prisms (e.g., three
#                     repos all entangled through a shared
#                     @spectral/garden distribution).
#   restriction_map — `restriction_map`. The cellular sheaf data
#                     the edge carries: source/target stalks (the
#                     state-projection slices) and the correlation
#                     ref (the typed restriction; math defers to
#                     @epistemologic/math).
#
# === The restriction_map shape ===
#
# The runtime altitude's cellular-sheaf data. Names the edge's
# substrate-altitude carrier; the realisation layer maps this
# into the math layer's `restriction` records when the eigenboard
# assembles the conductivity tensor.
#
#   source_stalk    — `shard_ref`. The state-projection slice at
#                     one endpoint of the edge (the "source"
#                     gen_prism's state shard ref). A stalk in
#                     cellular sheaf terminology: the data attached
#                     to a vertex.
#   target_stalk    — `shard_ref`. The state-projection slice at
#                     another endpoint. Together, source and target
#                     stalks name the edge's TWO ANCHOR endpoints
#                     even when peers.length > 2 — polyadic edges
#                     are CHAINS of pairwise restrictions sharing
#                     a common projection (the substrate's sheaf-
#                     theoretic reading: a polyadic edge is the
#                     equivalence class of pairwise restrictions
#                     under the shared projection).
#   correlation     — `ref`. The typed restriction — the cellular
#                     sheaf's F_{u ⊴ e} matrix block at the runtime
#                     altitude. The math layer's `weight: ref` in
#                     `shards/epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian.mirror`
#                     specializes this when the realisation layer
#                     assembles the conductivity tensor. The
#                     substrate names the obligation; @epistemologic/math
#                     discharges the magnitude at the eigenboard
#                     altitude.
#
# === The action surface ===
#
# Three load-bearing actions on entanglement edges. The substrate's
# question discipline (per [[architecture-error-as-question]]): each
# action returns either the new edge state or an `imperfect` carrier
# at the substrate-altitude verdict surface.
#
#   entangle(peers, map) -> entanglement_edge
#     Establish a new entanglement edge across the given peers with
#     the given restriction map. The substrate's discipline: this
#     does NOT register the edge with any supervisor (entanglement
#     is orthogonal to parentage); it produces the typed edge
#     record. The eigenboard query surface picks up the edge when
#     it observes the participating gen_prism state shards in its
#     next read. Body discharges by composing the peers and
#     restriction_map into the edge record.
#
#   disentangle(e, peer) -> entanglement_edge
#     Remove a peer from an entanglement edge. Returns a NEW edge
#     (peers minus the removed). If the resulting peers.length < 2
#     the edge is NO LONGER an entanglement (it carries only one
#     peer; the entanglement_arity_well_formed property surfaces
#     the opacity). Per the substrate's idempotent-on-miss
#     discipline (per registry.mirror's `remove`): if the peer is
#     not in the edge, returns `e` unchanged. Body discharges by
#     composing the edge minus the peer.
#
#   restriction_of(e, peer) -> imperfect
#     Project the restriction the named peer participates in across
#     this edge. Returns the substrate's three-state outcome per
#     @glass:
#
#       success(restriction_map) — peer is in the edge; the
#                                  restriction map is returned.
#       partial(...)              — the edge's state is mid-settle
#                                  (the source_stalk or target_stalk
#                                  shard is not yet readable at
#                                  @mirror/store); opacity_map names
#                                  the unsettled portion; kintsugi
#                                  re-reads to drive partial to
#                                  success.
#       failure(reason)           — peer is not in the edge. The
#                                  standard "not found" with reason
#                                  carrier per @glass.
#
#     Why `imperfect` and not a bare `restriction_map`: the
#     restriction_of query IS a question the eigenboard asks of the
#     edge — "what does this peer observe across this edge?" — and
#     the answer is verdict-laden, not a bare reference. Same
#     discipline as registry.lookup (per
#     `shards/spectral/registry.mirror` line 451): the question
#     carries its verdict at the type level.
#
# === Orthogonal to parent (the two-axis substrate graph) ===
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §4 and parent.mirror line
# 145-174: parent and entanglement are TWO ORTHOGONAL edge classes
# over the same gen_prism vocabulary:
#
#   parent_edge      directed; child → parent; one-per-child;
#                     ACYCLIC across the substrate; lifecycle-
#                     owning; the supervisor acts on this edge.
#   entanglement_edge polyadic; N peers; many-per-gen_prism;
#                     cyclic permitted; observational; sheaf
#                     restriction map at substrate altitude
#                     (cellular sheaf per [[Eigenboard is a sheaf]]).
#
# The two-axis substrate graph (this tick LANDS the second axis;
# parent landed at 9d905a2):
#
#   parent axis        ↑ supervision tree (lifecycle)
#   entanglement axis  ↔ peer correlation graph (observation)
#
# These two edge classes are NEVER conflated. A gen_prism's state
# surface is shaped BY ITS PARENT (lifecycle restart strategy fires
# under the parent's invariant) and OBSERVED BY ITS ENTANGLED PEERS
# (the eigenboard's restriction-map reading projects the state
# across entanglement edges). The runtime's substrate is the
# orthogonal sum of the two.
#
# === Mara-spawn example (the load-bearing case) ===
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §4:
#
#   - Mara spawns at `~/.mara/` to work on mirror's substrate.
#   - PARENT of Mara's spawn = `~/.mara/` gen_prism (lifecycle owner;
#     ~/.mara restart-strategy applies if Mara crashes).
#   - ENTANGLEMENT between Mara's spawn and mirror's repo gen_prism =
#     shared state projection (Mara's spawn reads mirror's substrate
#     shards; Mara's commits surface in mirror's eigenboard).
#
# The entanglement carries:
#
#   peers = [mara_spawn.identity, mirror_repo.identity]
#   restriction_map = {
#     source_stalk:  mara_spawn.state,     # Mara's current shard
#     target_stalk:  mirror_repo.state,    # mirror's current shard
#     correlation:   <ref to the shared substrate projection;
#                     the math layer's `weight` when assembled>
#   }
#
# A second Mara-spawn (sister Mara also working on mirror) joins the
# same entanglement edge through `entangle([mara_spawn, mirror_repo,
# mara_spawn_2], updated_map)` — the polyadicity names the
# n-gen_prism shared projection naturally.
#
# === Sheaf reading: NOT degenerate here — load-bearing ===
#
# Per parent.mirror line 175-195 and registry.mirror line 226-241:
# both shards surfaced a DEGENERATE sheaf reading (each parent_edge
# could be read as a restriction map; each registry_entry could be
# read as a restriction map) but did NOT carry the sheaf structure
# at the substrate altitude. The sheaf reading was FORWARD-PROMISED
# to this shard.
#
# This shard CARRIES the cellular sheaf restriction map as
# load-bearing substrate type — restriction_map IS the edge data;
# the eigenboard query surface assembles the conductivity tensor
# from these edges; @epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian's
# `sheaf_laplacian([restriction]) -> operator` is the realisation
# layer's discharge when the eigenboard pulls.
#
# Per [[Eigenboard is a sheaf]]:
#
#   "the substrate's eigenboard is a cellular sheaf on the
#    five-operation graph; restriction maps = conductivity tensor;
#    Reflection writes morphisms."
#
# The first half lands HERE (restriction maps live at entanglement;
# the conductivity tensor IS what the eigenboard query assembles
# from the population of entanglement edges). The second half
# (Reflection writes morphisms) lives at @kintsugi; the third
# (auditing the eigenboard's spectral state) lives at the
# eigenboard's query surface.
#
# === Honest partial discipline (per #306) ===
#
# The action bodies below all carry `\` obligation blocks — no
# fracture body emits any morphism in this tick. No `partials`
# declaration needed (per `dissonance_partials_match_ast_breadth`
# #306). Bodies discharge downstream when the consumer pulls
# realisation through `@facet/metalogue/materialize` (per the
# ouroboros pipeline in docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §5).
#
# === Cascade siblings (forward-promised, NOT landing this tick) ===
#
#   shards/spectral/portal.mirror         runtime side of portal
#                                          (gen_prism specialisation
#                                          carrying the frame stream;
#                                          form-side at
#                                          @mirror/spectral/portal).
#   shards/spectral/db.mirror             graph database engine; the
#                                          existing parentless ghost
#                                          (#198) GAINS @spectral
#                                          parent here; closed-engine
#                                          query surface on top of
#                                          the open @mirror/store
#                                          foundation.
#   shards/spectral/garden.mirror         vetted-corpus distribution;
#                                          existing parentless ghost
#                                          (#118) gains @spectral
#                                          parent when it lands.
#
# Conditional bilateral pair (substrate-pull candidate; STOP-
# condition 4 surfaced and forward-promised):
#
#   shards/epistemologic/property/entanglement_arity_well_formed.mirror
#     the declarative half — entanglement edges have at least
#     two peers (peers.length >= 2). Cycle reading: an edge with
#     one peer is not an edge; the substrate names the opacity.
#   shards/kintsugi/fracture/entanglement_singleton.mirror
#     the operational half — the fracture body that proposes
#     DROPPING a singleton-peers entanglement (the substrate's
#     idempotent-on-violation discipline: a violated arity edge
#     collapses to no edge; the peer's state surface retains
#     no entanglement participation across it).
#
# This bilateral pair is forward-promised because (a) the type
# declaration this tick lands is the FLOOR; (b) the bilateral
# pattern instance has the standard load-bearing weight of one
# tick per pair (per [[architecture-property-fracture-bilateral]]
# precedent: keyword/operator/symbol pairs each landed as their
# own tick); (c) the arity invariant is not LIFECYCLE-load-bearing
# (no supervisor acts on it; no runtime broken by a singleton-peers
# edge — the eigenboard simply reads no correlation across the
# degenerate edge). Lands as its own tick when the eigenboard's
# query surface or @spectral/db pulls the arity check as a real
# consumer.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md (the ouroboros
#               spec; §4 entanglement graph — the load-bearing
#               section this shard lifts; §9 open question 3 —
#               the cycle-in-entanglement question this tick
#               resolves; §8 cascade map).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[Eigenboard is a sheaf]] (the load-bearing
#                      architectural surface: entanglement edges
#                      ARE sheaf restriction maps at substrate
#                      altitude; this shard lifts the math layer's
#                      restriction primitive to the runtime altitude
#                      with uuid_spectral endpoints),
#                      [[architecture-shard-as-crdt]] (the
#                      restriction_map's stalks are shard_refs;
#                      the cyclic entanglement graph does not
#                      cycle shard ascent because shards are
#                      content-addressed; byte-equal shards
#                      collapse),
#                      [[architecture-connes-spectral-triple]]
#                      (the conductivity tensor IS A; the
#                      restriction map IS one block of D when
#                      assembled; this shard lives within Connes'
#                      framing at the substrate altitude),
#                      [[architecture-form-process-partition-at-family-root]]
#                      (entanglement is form-side observation;
#                      cyclic-permitted because observation
#                      doesn't transform — no kintsugi loop
#                      tension),
#                      [[architecture-error-as-question]]
#                      (restriction_of returns `imperfect`; the
#                      substrate's question discipline at the
#                      verdict surface),
#                      [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]
#                      (the rug-pull pattern: `restriction` was
#                      already named at @epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian;
#                      this tick lifts the same word as the
#                      runtime-altitude edge data with uuid_spectral
#                      endpoints — the 56th instance of the
#                      pattern, the first instance whose
#                      substrate-altitude lift is over a RUNTIME-
#                      EDGE-class subject).
# Related shards: spectral.mirror (the family root this glass is
#                  under; family-root altitude names entanglement
#                  at line 119-124),
#                 spectral/gen_prism.mirror (the worker primitive
#                  whose state surfaces participate in the
#                  restriction map; commit ae965ca),
#                 spectral/supervisor.mirror (the lifecycle-owner;
#                  ORTHOGONAL to this edge class; supervisor acts
#                  on parent_edge, NOT on entanglement_edge;
#                  commit 452ccb2),
#                 spectral/registry.mirror (the supervisor's
#                  parentage-side index whose typed surface stays
#                  at parentage; the sheaf reading was deferred TO
#                  HERE; commit 489211e),
#                 spectral/parent.mirror (the ORTHOGONAL sibling;
#                  binary directed acyclic lifecycle edge; this
#                  tick's polyadic cyclic observational edge is
#                  its orthogonal counterpart; commit 9d905a2),
#                 spectral/root.mirror (the parentless supervisor;
#                  participates in entanglement at the root
#                  altitude when @spectral/garden distributes
#                  across multiple roots; commit f145e48),
#                 uuid/spectral.mirror (the identity carrier at
#                  the edge's endpoints; peers are
#                  [uuid_spectral]; both halves load-bearing —
#                  active 48 bits for Fate's mycelial routing of
#                  cross-peer projection requests, dark 80 bits
#                  for endpoint identity),
#                 epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian.mirror (the
#                  math-altitude `restriction` primitive this
#                  shard's `restriction_map` lifts to runtime
#                  altitude; the eigenboard's realisation layer
#                  maps gen_prism uuid_spectrals to u32 vertex
#                  indices and assembles the operator from the
#                  population of runtime restriction_maps;
#                  `lambda_zero(operator) -> eigenvalue` reads
#                  the resulting algebraic connectivity),
#                 glass.mirror (the imperfect carrier for
#                  restriction_of's three-state outcome; the
#                  shard_ref carrier for stalk projections).
# Realisation reference: eigenboard query surface (MCP); when
#                  the eigenboard reads through to the conductivity
#                  tensor, the realisation layer composes
#                  restriction_maps across the gen_prism population
#                  into @epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian's
#                  `operator` and reads lambda_zero for algebraic
#                  connectivity.
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/spectral/entanglement.mirror` declares
# `@spectral/entanglement` and only that.

glass @spectral/entanglement {
  focus entanglement
  project entanglement
  split entanglement
  shift entanglement
  settle entanglement
}

# === restriction_map — the cellular sheaf edge data ===
#
# The runtime altitude's restriction map carried by an entanglement
# edge. Names the substrate-altitude edge data; the realisation
# layer (the eigenboard's conductivity-tensor assembly) maps this
# into the math layer's `restriction` records at
# `shards/epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian.mirror`.
#
#   source_stalk    — the source endpoint's state-projection slice.
#                     A `shard_ref` (uuid_spectral) into @mirror/store
#                     naming the source gen_prism's current state
#                     shard. The cellular sheaf's data attached to
#                     the source vertex.
#   target_stalk    — the target endpoint's state-projection slice.
#                     Same carrier type and discipline. Together
#                     source and target stalks name the edge's TWO
#                     ANCHOR endpoints even when an edge spans
#                     more than two peers (polyadic edges are
#                     equivalence classes of pairwise restrictions
#                     under a shared projection).
#   correlation     — the typed restriction. A `ref` to the
#                     restriction's substrate carrier; the math
#                     layer's `weight: ref` discharges the magnitude
#                     when the realisation layer assembles the
#                     conductivity tensor. The substrate names the
#                     obligation; @epistemologic/math discharges it.
type restriction_map = {
  source_stalk: shard_ref,
  target_stalk: shard_ref,
  correlation: ref,
}

# === entanglement_edge — one peer-correlation edge ===
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §4: the peer-correlation edge
# class. Orthogonal to parent_edge. Polyadic (peers list of
# arbitrary length >= 2; the arity property/fracture pair is
# forward-promised). Cyclic permitted (mutual entanglement is the
# common case; the substrate's content-addressed shard discipline
# guarantees no ascent cycles result).
#
#   peers           — the gen_prism identities participating in this
#                     edge. At least two; one peer is not an edge
#                     (the arity property/fracture pair surfaces the
#                     opacity when violated). Both halves of each
#                     uuid_spectral are load-bearing: the active 48
#                     bits route Fate's mycelial requests across the
#                     edge; the dark 80 bits identify each endpoint
#                     for eigenboard lookup.
#   restriction_map — the cellular sheaf data the edge carries.
type entanglement_edge = {
  peers: [uuid_spectral],
  restriction_map: restriction_map,
}

# === entangle — establish a new edge across peers ===
#
# Compose the peers and restriction map into an entanglement_edge.
# The substrate's discipline: this does NOT register the edge with
# any supervisor (entanglement is orthogonal to parentage; no
# lifecycle owner gains a child by this call); it produces the
# typed edge record. The eigenboard query surface picks up the
# edge when it observes the participating gen_prism state shards
# in its next read.
#
# Body discharges by composing the peers and restriction_map into
# the edge record. Stays a `\` obligation in this tick; concrete
# resolution lives at the realisation layer (via
# @facet/metalogue/materialize per the ouroboros pipeline).
entangle(peers: [uuid_spectral], map: restriction_map) -> entanglement_edge { \ }

# === disentangle — remove a peer from an edge ===
#
# Remove the named peer from the entanglement edge. Returns a NEW
# edge (peers minus the removed; the substrate's no-mutation
# discipline per registry.remove). If the peer is not in the
# edge, returns `e` unchanged (the substrate's idempotent-on-miss
# discipline per registry.remove line 168).
#
# If the resulting peers.length < 2 the edge is NO LONGER a
# well-formed entanglement (the arity property/fracture pair
# surfaces the opacity; the fracture body's substrate-pull-correct
# proposal is to DROP the degenerate edge). The type does NOT
# refuse the action — the substrate's three-state discipline is
# at the verdict surface (the arity property), not at the type
# carrier.
#
# Body discharges by composing the edge minus the peer. Stays a
# `\` obligation in this tick.
disentangle(e: entanglement_edge, peer: uuid_spectral) -> entanglement_edge { \ }

# === restriction_of — project the restriction for a peer ===
#
# Project the restriction map the named peer participates in across
# this edge. Returns the substrate's three-state outcome per
# @glass:
#
#   success(restriction_map) — peer is in the edge; the restriction
#                               map is returned. (For polyadic
#                               edges, the returned restriction
#                               map projects the shared correlation
#                               at the peer's endpoint.)
#   partial(...)              — the edge's state is mid-settle (the
#                               source_stalk or target_stalk shard
#                               is not yet readable at @mirror/store);
#                               opacity_map names the unsettled
#                               portion; kintsugi re-reads to drive
#                               partial to success.
#   failure(reason)           — peer is not in the edge. The
#                               standard "not found" with reason
#                               carrier per @glass.
#
# Same discipline as registry.lookup (per
# `shards/spectral/registry.mirror` line 451): the question carries
# its verdict at the type level. The eigenboard's MCP query surface
# reads through this action when it asks "what does this gen_prism
# observe across this entanglement edge?"
#
# Body discharges by reading the edge's peers, discriminating on
# byte-equality of the named peer's dark 80 bits, lifting the
# matching restriction_map into success / partial / failure. Stays
# a `\` obligation in this tick.
restriction_of(e: entanglement_edge, peer: uuid_spectral) -> imperfect { \ }

out @spectral/entanglement
out restriction_map
out entanglement_edge
out entangle
out disentangle
out restriction_of
